<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dinmukhamed Kunaev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dinmukhamed Kunaev was a Kazakh Communist leader who headed the Communist Party of the Kazakh SSR from 1960 to 1962 and again from 1964 to 1986, and sat on the Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a>. A close ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-brezhnev">Leonid Brezhnev</a>, he oversaw the growth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Almaty</a> and of industry in the republic. His replacement by the Russian Gennady Kolbin in December 1986 set off the Jeltoqsan protests.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dinmukhamed-kunaev.jpg" alt="Dinmukhamed Kunaev" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kunayev_(cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kunayev was born on 12 January 1912 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Verny</a>, now Almaty, the son of a Kazakh clerk. He studied at the Institute of Non-Ferrous and Fine Metallurgy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and by 1939 was engineer-in-chief of the Pribalkhashatroi mine, when he joined the Communist Party. From April 1942 to 1946 he was deputy chairman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-peoples-commissars">Council of People's Commissars</a> of the Kazakh SSR and oversaw the setting up of factories evacuated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>. He was president of the Academy of Sciences of the Kazakh SSR from 1952 and chairman of the Council of Ministers from 1955. His rise was closely tied to that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-brezhnev">Leonid Brezhnev</a>, with whom he formed a friendship that lasted until Brezhnev's death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>On 19 January 1960 Kunayev was elected first secretary of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, the first Kazakh to hold the post. He supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgin-lands-campaign">Virgin Lands campaign</a>, which brought a large influx of Russian settlers. In 1962 he was dismissed after disagreeing with Khrushchev's plans to transfer lands in southern Kazakhstan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. He returned as first secretary in 1964, when Khrushchev was ousted, and held the post for 22 more years. He became a full member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> in 1971.</p><p>In his second term the Virgin Lands Territory was abolished, new regions such as Turgai and Jezkazgan were formed in the north, and areas given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> were returned, so that the republic's territory grew for the first time since 1936. Supporters credit him with the building of oil refineries at Pavlodar and Chimkent and with backing Kazakh writers whose books were refused elsewhere.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At the 16th Congress of the Kazakh party in February 1986 Kunayev was criticised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nursultan-nazarbayev">Nursultan Nazarbayev</a>, chairman of the Council of Ministers. He was removed under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a>, who accused him of corruption, and on 16 December 1986 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> replaced him with Gennady Kolbin, an ethnic Russian who had never lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>. The decision set off three days of street rioting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Almaty</a>, called Jeltoqsan, or December in Kazakh. Kunayev died of a heart attack on 22 August 1993 at Akshi and was buried at the Kensai cemetery in Alma-Ata on 25 August.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dinmukhamed Kunaev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Dinmukhamed Kunaev</b> led the republic from 1964 to 1986.</blockquote><a href="/state/kazakh-ssr">Kazakh SSR</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Almaty</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>. Died in Aqshi, in what is now <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.24,76.92&amp;year=1964" aria-label="Almaty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6404 629 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dinmukhamed Kunaev"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6854 899h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6854 899h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.24,76.92&amp;year=1964">What was here in 1964? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
